          }
        ],
        "conclusion": "Patient has an elevated Prostate Health Index (phi = 47.8) in this synthetic test result, indicating an elevated probability (36.0% - 55.0%) of clinically significant prostate cancer (Gleason Score >= 7) on biopsy.\n\nClinical Interpretation: The Prostate Health Index is calculated via the formula: phi = ([-2]proPSA / Free PSA) * sqrt(Total PSA). A phi value >= 36.0 is associated with a 36.0% to 55.0% probability of cancer on biopsy. In men aged 50 and older with total PSA in the 4.0 to 10.0 ng/mL diagnostic gray zone, this elevated score provides strong clinical justification for mpMRI and targeted prostate biopsy. \u2022 Multiparametric prostate MRI (mpMRI): 3-Tesla pelvic mpMRI with PI-RADS scoring. \u2022 Urology consult: Referral for MRI-fusion targeted and systematic transperineal or transrectal prostate biopsy.\n\nRecommended Next Steps:\n\u2022 Multiparametric prostate MRI (mpMRI): 3-Tesla pelvic mpMRI with PI-RADS scoring.\n\u2022 Urology consult: Referral for MRI-fusion targeted and systematic transperineal or transrectal prostate biopsy.\n\nMethodology: Total PSA, Free PSA, and [-2]proPSA are quantitatively measured in serum using Beckman Coulter Access chemiluminescent immunoassays calibrated against WHO standards. The phi score is derived mathematically.\n\nLimitations: phi results should be evaluated in conjunction with digital rectal examination (DRE), prostate volume, family history, and clinical risk factors.\n\nLaboratory Director Authorization: Dr. Keith Carlson, MD, FCAP | Electronically Signed | CLIA ID: 00D1122334"
      },
      "request": {
        "method": "POST",
        "url": "DiagnosticReport"
